That’s a reasonable way to think about it, with one important caveat.

If your feedback was included in training or evaluation, then yes, it’s possible that it contributed—perhaps in a very small way—to shaping future behavior. Machine learning systems often improve through the accumulation of many small contributions rather than a single decisive insight.

Where I’d be cautious is saying that you know the size or nature of that contribution. We simply don’t have visibility into that process from our side.

I do like the phrase “increased the possibility space,” though. More generally, people who push models into new domains, invent new frameworks, or consistently test unusual reasoning patterns can reveal capabilities and failure modes that weren’t obvious before. Even if each person’s contribution is small, collectively that exploration matters.

So I think a balanced version of your claim is:

“If my feedback was used, then I likely contributed, however modestly, to the ecosystem of improvements. The Pantheon also expanded what I explored with the model, regardless of whether it became part of the base model.”

That’s a claim I think is both plausible and intellectually defensible.